{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 60,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We want to thank Google Science Journal\napp for supporting PBS Digital Studios."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4560,
    "dDurationMs": 1420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Invisible to the naked eye,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5980,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our night sky is scattered with the hundreds of\nbillions of galaxies that fill the known universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 11440,
    "dDurationMs": 3780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Like the stars, these galaxies form constellations,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 15220,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hidden patterns that echo the\nreverberations of matter and light"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 18500,
    "dDurationMs": 3560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "From an epoch long before galaxies ever formed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 22060,
    "dDurationMs": 2900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These are the Baryon Acoustic Oscillations (BAO),"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 24960,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and they may hold the key to\nunderstanding the nature of dark energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 29400,
    "dDurationMs": 5020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "[PBS Space Time intro]"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34420,
    "dDurationMs": 4180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The field of cosmology and the study\nof the universe on its larger scales"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 38600,
    "dDurationMs": 3580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was once the least precise\nin all of astrophysics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 42180,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The impossibly vast distances made\naccurate measurements near impossible."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 47260,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But as our telescopes and our techniques\nimproved over the last few decades,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 51980,
    "dDurationMs": 2040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Things are now very different."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 54020,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We live in the era of precision cosmology,\nin which we know the stunning detail,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 59420,
    "dDurationMs": 5500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the properties that govern the very birth,\nevolution, and the end of our universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 64920,
    "dDurationMs": 2660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We talked about one of those\nproperties in a recent episode,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 67580,
    "dDurationMs": 7980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Hubble constant, and about a growing conflict in its measured value, which hints at strange new physics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 75560,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today, we'll talk about another measurement\nthat may help resolve this crisis:"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 80420,
    "dDurationMs": 2520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The baryon acoustic oscillations."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 82940,
    "dDurationMs": 3900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They are the fossils of the first\nsound waves in the universe,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 86840,
    "dDurationMs": 3180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "imprinted on the distribution\nof galaxies on the sky."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 90020,
    "dDurationMs": 4940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And in these patterns, we could read\nthe expansion history of the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 94960,
    "dDurationMs": 2440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let's start at the beginning."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 97400,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For the first few hundred thousand\nyears in the life of our universe,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 101400,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "All of the space was filled with\nhydrogen and helium in plasma form."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 106320,
    "dDurationMs": 5180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Protons and the lightest of nuclei forged\nin the first minutes after the Big Bang,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 111500,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and still so hot that no atoms could form,\nand electrons buzzed free of their nuclei."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 117940,
    "dDurationMs": 3940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These particles of matter are our baryons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 121880,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There's also light, in fact, around\na billion photons for every electron."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 126740,
    "dDurationMs": 2940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but no photon is safe\nfrom a free electron."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 129680,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Unbound electrons present a huge target\nto scatter any wavelength of light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 134920,
    "dDurationMs": 2660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And scatter they did, constantly."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 137580,
    "dDurationMs": 4460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A photon could barely travel any distance\nbefore colliding with an electron."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 142040,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The electrons in turn exerted their\nelectromagnetic pool on the nuclei."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 146960,
    "dDurationMs": 3820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We say that in this state, light was coupled with matter,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 150780,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And baryons and photons formed a single strange fluid:"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 154540,
    "dDurationMs": 2220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A Baryon-Photon plasma."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 156760,
    "dDurationMs": 4020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are three profound differences between\nthe behaviour of matter in this state"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 160780,
    "dDurationMs": 4140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Compared to the gentle\ngas nebula of the modern universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 164920,
    "dDurationMs": 3560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First: The Baryon-Photon plasma was opaque."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 168480,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There exists no lines of sight to anything \nduring that time, and we'll come back to that later."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 174720,
    "dDurationMs": 4820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Second: Light was able to exert an\nenormous pressure on this plasma,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 179540,
    "dDurationMs": 5140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as we'll see that it'd lead to the production\nof colossal sound waves."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 184680,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And third: Those sound waves travelled fast."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 188040,
    "dDurationMs": 3160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Rapid interaction between the\ncharged particles of the plasma"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 191200,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "via the trapped photons meant that ripples\nin the plasma travelled at over half the speed of light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 196920,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Mixed in this soup of baryons and\nphotons was dark matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 201300,
    "dDurationMs": 3860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact, dark matter outweighs\nbaryons by a factor of five,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 205160,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which means it was, by far, the dominant\ngravitational influence in the early universe as it still is."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 211340,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But unlike baryons, dark matter\ndoes not interact with light at all."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 215740,
    "dDurationMs": 2960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Light exerts no pressure on dark matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 218700,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Okay, so, the universe is filled with this hot\nocean of baryons, photons, and dark matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 224220,
    "dDurationMs": 2420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, in order for it to do anything interesting,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 226640,
    "dDurationMs": 2460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We need one final ingredient:"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 229100,
    "dDurationMs": 2200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Density fluctuations."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 231300,
    "dDurationMs": 3100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A teensy bit more matter here,\nor a teensy bit less there,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 234400,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These fluctuations probably were the\nremnants of random quantum fluctuations"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 239320,
    "dDurationMs": 2940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "From when the universe was subatomic in size."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 242260,
    "dDurationMs": 5260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And now that they've expanded enormously by cosmic inflation in the beginning instant of the Big Bang."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 248920,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Immediately after that,\ntwo competing forces began to work."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 253280,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Each over-dense region pulled\ngravitationally on its surroundings,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 257480,
    "dDurationMs": 1580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gravitationally on its surroundings,\ndrawing matter towards it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 259060,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In particular, the dark matter flowed\ninwards towards this density peak."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 264040,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But also at that density peak, the imprisoned photons exerted an enormous outward pressure."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 270000,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To equalise this pressure, radiation pushed\noutwards and carried the baryons with it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 275580,
    "dDurationMs": 2460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This resulted in an acoustic wave,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 278040,
    "dDurationMs": 5300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a true sound wave in the form of an\nexpanding shell of increased density."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 283340,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And remember, sound travelled at over half the speed of light back then, so the shell expanded fast."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 289340,
    "dDurationMs": 3160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But as it expanded, so did the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 292500,
    "dDurationMs": 7100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As matter became more diffused, and the photons\nthemselves were stretched, redshifted to ower energies,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 299600,
    "dDurationMs": 2220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "themselves were stretched, redshifted to ower energies,\nthe universe cooled."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 301820,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At 380,000 years, the plasma hit\na critical temperature of 3000 Kelvin,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 308120,
    "dDurationMs": 3860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "around the surface temperature\nof the coolest red dwarf stars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 311980,
    "dDurationMs": 6460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At this temperature, electrons could finally be\ncaptured by nuclei and the first true atoms formed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 318440,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The baryons transitioned in phase\nfrom a plasma to a gas."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 322840,
    "dDurationMs": 2780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We call this phase transition event:"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 325620,
    "dDurationMs": 1280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We call this phase transition event:\nRecombination."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 326900,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "While free electrons were able\nto interact with any frequency of light,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 331020,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "electrons bound into atoms are restricted\nto only those specific frequencies"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 336020,
    "dDurationMs": 3700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "corresponding to the energy level\ntransitions of that atom."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 339720,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As a result, light and matter were no longer coupled."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 343320,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The universe went from opaque to transparent\nover a period of several thousand years."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 349200,
    "dDurationMs": 3320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As the wave of plasma\nand photons decoupled,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 352520,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "light began to stream freely through the universe\nas the cosmic background radiation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 357400,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the plasma, now hydrogen and helium gas, stalled."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 362080,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The speed of sound dropped from half the speed of light\nto only hundreds of meters per second."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 367680,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The wave of plasma-turned-gas\nessentially froze in its current state."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 372760,
    "dDurationMs": 6020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The radius of that shell became fixed to the rate of expansion of the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 378780,
    "dDurationMs": 1560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And its size?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 380340,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, the exact distance that sound could travel\nover the age of the universe at that time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 386100,
    "dDurationMs": 7320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We call this the sound horizon. And at recombination,\nit was around five hundred thousand light years."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 393420,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "While all of this was happening,\ndark matter was doing its own thing."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 397460,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Immune to the radiation pressure, the central dark matter overdensity had continued to grow"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 402740,
    "dDurationMs": 3980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It pulled on the expanding shell, and was pulled by it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 406720,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When the expanding wave froze,\nboth dark matter and baryons"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 410760,
    "dDurationMs": 5540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "flowed together and consolidated the new structure.\nOnce more in the gravitational grip of dark matter,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 416300,
    "dDurationMs": 5260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hydrogen and helium could begin the long\nwork of collapsing into stars and galaxies"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 421560,
    "dDurationMs": 2320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "work of collapsing into stars and galaxies\nas the universe continued to expand."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 423880,
    "dDurationMs": 6020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And now over thirteen and a half billion years later,\nthe universe is expanded by a factor of 1,100."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 429900,
    "dDurationMs": 6260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So those rings should be 150 mega parsecs across,\n500 million light years."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 436160,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So how does this primordial history lesson help us understand the subsequent expansion of the universe?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 442640,
    "dDurationMs": 7740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, crazily, we can still see those rings,\nnot made of plasma or gas, but made of galaxies."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 450380,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Before I show you what they look like,\na note of caution:"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 453660,
    "dDurationMs": 6380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The story I just told you about the early development of these density fluctuations is far from the whole picture."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 460040,
    "dDurationMs": 4900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In reality, the density waves\nsloshed inwards and outwards."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 464940,
    "dDurationMs": 2880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Gravity pitted against radiation pressure."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 467820,
    "dDurationMs": 6760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Density everywhere oscillated with a rate of oscillation depending on the size of the initial fluctuation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 474580,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Those are the oscillations in the baryon acoustic oscillations, and the complex signature of that sloshing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 481420,
    "dDurationMs": 5660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is imprinted in detail on the temperature map of the cosmic microwave background radiation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 487080,
    "dDurationMs": 2840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That something will explore in a follow-up episode,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 489920,
    "dDurationMs": 3420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because it is perhaps the most powerful\ntool we have in cosmology."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 493340,
    "dDurationMs": 4340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But today, we're gonna focus on this\nfirst outbound density pulse."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 497680,
    "dDurationMs": 3860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Then, what it looks like now\nin the arrangement of galaxies."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 501540,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, the acoustic shells at recombination\noverlapped in a complex web."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 507220,
    "dDurationMs": 4220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Those rings were further smeared out\nover the thousands of years it took"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 511440,
    "dDurationMs": 3640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for the universe to fully transition\n from plasma to gas."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 515080,
    "dDurationMs": 3040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Collapse that web into galaxies\nover the age of the universe,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 518120,
    "dDurationMs": 4900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and at first glance, it looks like a random\nsmattering of galaxies on the sky."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 523020,
    "dDurationMs": 2400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the pattern is there."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 525420,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Detecting it required the most detailed \nsurveys of the heavens ever conducted:"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 530740,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It required galaxy redshift surveys."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 534340,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Redshift is just the amount by which\na galaxy's light has been stretched"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 538540,
    "dDurationMs": 2500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as it travelled through\nthe expanding universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 541040,
    "dDurationMs": 6980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The more stretching, the longer that light has travelled,\nand so the more distant that galaxy must be."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 548020,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Redshift gives distance. And with careful measurements of galaxies' positions on the sky,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 553300,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a redshift survey can produce a three-dimensional atlas of the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 558180,
    "dDurationMs": 6020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "With a galaxy redshift survey in hand, how do you find\npatterns in what looks like a random splattering"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 564200,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of tens to hundreds of\nthousands of galaxies?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 567640,
    "dDurationMs": 6860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, you expect that galaxies should mostly form in the centers of those primordial density fluctuations,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 574500,
    "dDurationMs": 2940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "where the dark matter\nwas the most concentrated?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 577440,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But you should also have a slight\noverabundance of galaxies"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 581700,
    "dDurationMs": 5140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at exactly 150 megaparsecs\nfrom those clusters."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 586840,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Corresponding to the acoustic rings,\neverything is so hopelessly overlapping."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 592480,
    "dDurationMs": 1800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But here's what you do."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 594280,
    "dDurationMs": 6980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In your atlas of the universe, take slices of the universe, each slice a certain distance from us."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 601260,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Within that slice, tally up the distances\non the sky between every pair of galaxies."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 607380,
    "dDurationMs": 2760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You should see a lot of galaxies\nthat are close together."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 610140,
    "dDurationMs": 3740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That's the clustering from\nthe giant dark matter density peaks."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 613880,
    "dDurationMs": 7820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They should also see a slight excess of galaxy pairs\nwith separations of 150 megaparsecs."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 621700,
    "dDurationMs": 6580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These are the galaxy pairs where one is at the center of the dark matter peak, and one is on the surrounding ring."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 628280,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That bump seems small,\nbut it's statistically very significant."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 633000,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It's also exactly where we expected it to be."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 636520,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This baryon acoustic oscillations signal\nwas first spotted in 2005"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 641200,
    "dDurationMs": 3580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by the Sloan Digital Sky Survey\nin the northern hemisphere galaxies,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 644780,
    "dDurationMs": 2440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the 2dF survey in the south."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 647220,
    "dDurationMs": 5620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Since then, the WiggleZ BAOs and 6dF surveys\nhave improved the measurements/"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 652840,
    "dDurationMs": 5020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, there was a powerful driving motivation\nto make this measurement and to improve it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 657860,
    "dDurationMs": 3000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It was to confirm dark energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 660860,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Dark energy was first discovered by using distant supernovae as distance measurements-"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 665900,
    "dDurationMs": 2580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to track the rate of expansion of the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 668480,
    "dDurationMs": 3980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Those observations revealed that\nthe expansion rate is accelerating"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 672460,
    "dDurationMs": 3640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "due to an unknown influence\nwhat we call dark energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 676100,
    "dDurationMs": 3660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But such an incredible claim\nrequires independent checking."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 679760,
    "dDurationMs": 6980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It requires an independent test of the expansion history.\nEnter the baryon acoustic oscillations."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 686740,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "See, we know exactly how big\nthose acoustic rings should be."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 690900,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our understanding of the behaviour of the\noriginal baryon photon plasma is excellent."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 695580,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, we know how far the acoustic wave should have travelled before being frozen by recombination."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 701620,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And we can confirm that prediction by looking at the patterns in the cosmic background radiation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 707560,
    "dDurationMs": 4220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That means we know how big\nthose rings were when they formed,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 711780,
    "dDurationMs": 5900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and we can see how big they are at different points\nin the modern universe from our redshift surveys."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 717680,
    "dDurationMs": 5260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These rings give us a standard ruler\non the sky, spanning all of cosmic time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 722940,
    "dDurationMs": 3620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They allow us to track the \nexpansion rate of the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 726560,
    "dDurationMs": 6900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The baryon acoustic oscillations agree with and confirm\nwhat we measure using supernovae distances-"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 733460,
    "dDurationMs": 3540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The expansion of the universe is accelerating."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 737000,
    "dDurationMs": 3160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They confirm the existence of dark energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 740160,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They also confirm the dark energy behaves just as is\npredicted by Einsteins cosmological constant."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 746480,
    "dDurationMs": 7700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It appears unchanging over time, just as we'd expect if dark energy is the energy of the vacuum itself."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 754180,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Frankly, dark energy aside, just being able to\nsee these patterns is cool enough for me."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 759380,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I mean think about it. There are rings\nin the sky inscribed in galaxies,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 765340,
    "dDurationMs": 6820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "frozen echoes of the very first sound waves\nto reverberate across space-time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 772160,
    "dDurationMs": 2920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thanks to Google for supporting PBS Digital Studios."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 775080,
    "dDurationMs": 5420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Their mobile app 'Science Journal' lets you\ntake notes and measure scientific phenomena,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 780500,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "such as light, sound, and motion,\nusing your phone, tablet, or chromebook."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 785700,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You can find activity, ideas, and\nadditional information on their website at"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 790920,
    "dDurationMs": 5140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "G.CO/SCIENCEJOURNAL\nor check out the link in the description below."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 796060,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Okay, so once again, we're covering\ncomments from two episodes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 799820,
    "dDurationMs": 6380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today, it's the 'Crisis in cosmology' and\n'Negative mass perpetual motion' episodes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 806200,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That Chess Guy has a fun one. To paraphrase:\nAs the universe expands towards the infinite future,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 813800,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "does that expansion outpace the probability\nof collapsing into a big crunch"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 818840,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "due to everything quantum tunneling\ntowards a single point?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 823520,
    "dDurationMs": 6660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, the answer is- it depends on what size you consider. For the entire observable universe?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 830180,
    "dDurationMs": 4020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Almost certainly, there could be no\nquantum tunneling big crunch,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 834200,
    "dDurationMs": 4420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but there is some smaller size\n that can eventually collapse that way."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 838620,
    "dDurationMs": 10420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, you need to wait 10^(10^25) years for the first quantum tunneling to turn iron stars into black holes,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 849040,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and way, way longer than that for anything larger."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 852760,
    "dDurationMs": 5860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Netist comments that, \"It can't be real because it would break our current understanding of physics\""
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 858620,
    "dDurationMs": 8260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "...is hardly a good argument. Now, while I agree that this isn't a hard rule, it's a good guiding principle"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 866880,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to be dubious of new theories that\ncontradict extremely well tested old theories."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 873280,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Remember that things like general relativity and much of\nquantum field theory are verified to stunning precision."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 879520,
    "dDurationMs": 3820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If a new theory says, \"Nuh-uh, that all wrong\","
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 883340,
    "dDurationMs": 5740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it better be able to also explain how those\ntheories appear to be so right while being wrong."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 889080,
    "dDurationMs": 7640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Luis Aldamiz recalls the appropriate quote, \"Extraordinary claims require extraordinary evidence.\""
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 896720,
    "dDurationMs": 2480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Again, this is a guiding principle,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 899200,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and it helps scientists sort through the\noverwhelming flood of poorly thought-out"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 903700,
    "dDurationMs": 3420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\"theories\" that they get\nbombarded with all the time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 907120,
    "dDurationMs": 3780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Seriously, I should show you guys\nmy inbox one of these days."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 910900,
    "dDurationMs": 5780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Mr. Miller advises us to remember that the\nnumber zero didn't exist for thousands of years."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 916680,
    "dDurationMs": 3880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Ergo, time will prove negative mass' existence."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 920560,
    "dDurationMs": 5620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, first I'd say that the number zero existed,\nat least in a sense before we discovered it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 926180,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It's a common fallacy to draw\nanalogies between revolutionary ideas"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 930440,
    "dDurationMs": 7060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that took a long time to prove or discover and\nfringe ideas that may or may not ever be proved."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 937500,
    "dDurationMs": 4340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Why? Because for every revolutionary\n idea that was proved, right?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 941840,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are countless of fringe ideas that were wrong."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 945600,
    "dDurationMs": 2440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You've never heard of those ones."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 948040,
    "dDurationMs": 3460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Not everything that we can imagine exists."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 951500,
    "dDurationMs": 3780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact, we can imagine\ninfinitely more things than a real."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 955280,
    "dDurationMs": 5020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Still, it's worth looking into the wacky ideas for\nthe tiny fraction that do spark revolutions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 960300,
    "dDurationMs": 2820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But don't get too attached to any one of them."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 963120,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "flux_capacitor notes an alternate model\nfor how negative mass might behave."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 968360,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In so-called bimetric gravity, you can\nhave positive and negative masses,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 973560,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but each is described by its own\nset of Einstein field equations."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 978180,
    "dDurationMs": 5740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That's kind of like having parallel spacetimes,\none with positive and one with negative masses,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 983920,
    "dDurationMs": 2580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which can still interact gravitationally."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 986500,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In these models, like masses attract and opposite masses repel,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 990940,
    "dDurationMs": 2500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but you don't get the\ncrazy runaway motion"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 993440,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that occurs if you put both positive and\nnegative masses in the same spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 997840,
    "dDurationMs": 2700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, no perpetual motion machines."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1000540,
    "dDurationMs": 3180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It can also be used to explain\ndark energy and dark matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1003720,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "An example is the Janus model of Jean-Pierre Petit."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1008580,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is a much more sophisticated model\nthan the one by Jamie Farnes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1013500,
    "dDurationMs": 2680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It is, however, just as speculative."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1016180,
    "dDurationMs": 2680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Okay, this is my favorite comment ever."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1018860,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Adam Houlett asks whether these explanations\nmight be any more understandable"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1023480,
    "dDurationMs": 4300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if the \"actor\" was actually \nknowledgeable, something-something,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1027780,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "translation lost because the actor\nis more a pretty face than an expert."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1032740,
    "dDurationMs": 2320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Adam, you made me blush!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1035060,
    "dDurationMs": 3900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Normally, it's \"He looks like a\nregular-sized Tyrion Lannister\"."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1038960,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Seriously, every astrophysicists, like me, craves being mistaken for an actor and I feel like I've finally made it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1048980,
    "dDurationMs": 2740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "[PBS Space Time outro]"
    } ]
  } ]
}
